f="/unity-theory/research-status/">Research</a><a href="/unity-theory/documents/">Documents</a><a href="/unity-theory/articles/">Articles</a><a href="/unity-theory/cite/">Cite</a><a href="/unity-theory/references/">Sources</a><a href="/unity-theory/search/">Search</a></nav> </header> <main id="main" tabindex="-1"> <section class="hero shell" aria-labelledby="research-question"> <div><p class="eyebrow">A question about organization</p> <h1 id="research-question">How do parts become<br class="desktop-break" /> a <em>whole?</em></h1> <p class="hero-lede">Unity Theory asks how arrangement and activity support persistent organization—and make further organization possible.</p> <a class="button" href="/unity-theory/start/">Start with the idea <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a> <p class="reading-note">A short introduction · No technical background needed</p> <p class="reading-note" data-editorial-state="DOC-HOME">Publication: Draft · private preview. Source fidelity: Stale — rereview required.</p> </div> </section> <section class="reading-section shell"><div class="margin-label"><span>01 / The idea</span><p>From something familiar<br>to a research question.</p></div><div class="prose"><div data-canonical-body="DOC-HOME"><h2 id="begin-with-a-wave">Begin with a wave</h2>\n<p>Watch a ripple cross water. A crest moves across the surface as the water moves locally. The visible pattern and the material carrying it give us two ways to describe one event. <a href="/unity-theory/examples/water/">Explore the wave</a>; the background physics is explained by <a href="/unity-theory/references/#BIB-0060">NOAA's wave explanation</a>.</p>\n<h2 id="arrangement-and-activity-belong-together">Arrangement and activity belong together</h2>\n<p>A string's fixed ends constrain its motion. A molecule's connections constrain its collective behaviour. A cell keeps its organization through ongoing exchanges with its surroundings.</p>\n<p>Recursive Resonant Geometry, or <strong>RRG</strong>, asks how relationships and activity support one another. Its <strong>geometry</strong> means components, connections, boundaries and constraints. Its <strong>mode structure</strong> means the organized ways those parts change and respond over time.</p>\n<h2 id="a-whole-can-become-a-part">A whole can become a part</h2>\n<p>In the RRG proposal, compatible active parts may form a persistent whole. That whole can participate in another organization while activity continues inside it. Existing structures can also change the conditions for what happens next: a star makes new reaction conditions; living systems change their chemical surroundings.</p>\n<p>The wave introduces a distinction, rather than proving this entire cycle. The examples have different physical mechanisms. The research question is whether a useful, predictive organizing principle connects them.</p>\n<p><a href="/unity-theory/start/">Read the full introduction</a> or <a href="/unity-theory/concepts/">explore the concepts</a>.</p></div><figure class="beginner-diagram" data-beginner-diagram="DOC-HOME"><svg viewBox="0 0 360 370" role="img" aria-labelledby="diagram-DOC-HOME-title diagram-DOC-HOME-description"><title id="diagram-DOC-HOME-title">Parts, a whole, and further organization</title><desc id="diagram-DOC-HOME-description">Compatible active parts can couple into a collective organization. A persistent whole may become a useful unit in further combinations. The surroundings constrain the process, and existing organization may change those surroundings. This is the RRG research proposal, not a guaranteed sequence.</desc><rect class="diagram-environment" x="14" y="14" width="332" height="342" rx="22"/><text x="180" y="42">Surroundings &amp; conditions</text>\n      <g class="diagram-node"><rect x="57" y="64" width="246" height="56" rx="8"/><text x="180" y="89">Compatible active parts</text><text class="diagram-subtitle" x="180" y="109">different parts can play different roles</text></g>\n      <path class="diagram-link" d="M180 120 V147"/><path class="diagram-arrowhead" d="m175 142 5 6 5-6"/>\n      <g class="diagram-node"><rect x="57" y="151" width="246" height="68" rx="8"/><text x="180" y="178">Collective organization</text><text class="diagram-subtitle" x="180" y="200">arrangement ↔ activity</text></g>\n      <path class="diagram-link" d="M180 219 V245"/><path class="diagram-arrowhead" d="m175 240 5 6 5-6"/>\n      <g class="diagram-node"><rect x="57" y="249" width="246" height="60" rx="8"/><text x="180" y="276">A useful unit</text><text class="diagram-subtitle" x="180" y="297">in further combinations</text></g>\n      <path class="diagram-feedback" d="M303 277 C333 277 333 84 303 84"/><path class="diagram-arrowhead" d="m309 79-6 5 6 5"/><text class="diagram-subtitle" x="180" y="337">organization can change conditions</text></svg><figcaption>A proposed cycle: active parts → collective organization → a useful unit for further combinations. Conditions affect formation; existing organization may change conditions. Illustration, not a measurement.</figcaption></figure></div></section> <section class="pathways shell" aria-labelledby="explore-title"> <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Choose your depth</p><h2 id="explore-title">Follow the question.</h2></div> <div class="pathway-grid"> <a class="pathway" href="/unity-theory/examples/string/"><span class="pathway-number">01 / Constraints</span><h3>A string's possible motion <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></h3><p>Fixed ends, physical conditions and the patterns they support.</p><span class="pathway-state">Explore the string</span></a> <a class="pathway" href="/unity-theory/examples/molecule/"><span class="pathway-number">02 / A whole</span><h3>More than a bag of atoms <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></h3><p>Connections constrain collective behaviour while the parts remain active.</p><span class="pathway-state">Explore the molecule</span></a> <a class="pathway" href="/unity-theory/examples/life-environment/"><span class="pathway-number">03 / Possibilities</span><h3>Life changes conditions <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></h3><p>An existing organization can change what becomes possible around it.</p><span class="pathway-state">Explore life and environment</span></a> </div> <p class="reading-note"><a href="/unity-theory/examples/">All six examples</a> · <a href="/unity-theory/concepts/">Explore the source meanings</a></p> </section> <section class="open-question shell"><p class="eyebrow">The work ahead</p><h2>A framework to investigate.</h2><div class="prose" data-source-projection="DOC-STATUS"><h2 id="open-extensions-to-prove--not-definitions">Open extensions to prove — not definitions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Whether the four known fundamental interactions are manifestations of deeper scale-dependent resonant geometry.</li>\n<li>Whether gravity is an emergent higher-scale geometric/mode regime.</li>\n<li>Whether cosmological background evolution selects which resonant geometries are accessible.</li>\n<li>Whether one cross-domain mathematical invariant or promotion criterion exists.</li>\n<li>Whether RRG yields a novel prediction beyond existing frameworks.</li>\n<li>Whether an RRG-inspired AI architecture gives measurable efficiency/generalization advantages.</li>\n</ol></div><p><a href="/unity-theory/research-status/">Read current research status</a></p></section> <section class="shell edition-note"><p class="eyebrow">The current edition</p><p>Core definitions, source-reported evidence and open extensions remain distinct. <a href="/unity-theory/references/">Find the sources</a> and <a href="/unity-theory/research-status/">follow the current status</a>.</p><p class="reading-note">Public introduction: author approval pending.</p></section> </main> <aside class="source-note shell" aria-label="Source availability"> <span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> <div><strong>Current sources available</strong> <p>13 supplied files checked against the recorded edition. Website expl